Looking more carefully at the validation logic, I found a real bug! Let me trace through it:

At line 538: `if end == -1: end = None`

At line 545: `if start is not None and end is not None and end != -1 and start > end:`

The issue is that by line 545, `end` has already been converted from `-1` to `None` (if it was `-1`). So `end != -1` will always be `True` when `end is not None`. But more importantly, if `end == -1` was passed and converted to `None`, then `end is not None` will be `False`, so the whole condition short-circuits and the `start > end` check won't happen.

